---
import PageLayout from "@/layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import { site } from "@/data/site";

// R2 — the whole-machine-layer colophon. Documentation register: factual,
// third-person, complete. Dates are fixed constants (never build time) so the
// page's metadata is byte-stable across daily cron builds.
const DATE_PUBLISHED = "2026-07-04";
const DATE_MODIFIED = "2026-07-04";

const description =
  "How the machine layer of sjarmak.ai works: the daily digest pipeline, local text-to-speech, the knowledge sync, the concepts registry and graph, retrieval lanes, and the sync heartbeat.";

const techArticleLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "TechArticle",
  headline: "Colophon: how the machine layer of sjarmak.ai works",
  description,
  author: {
    "@type": "Person",
    name: site.name,
    url: site.url,
  },
  datePublished: DATE_PUBLISHED,
  dateModified: DATE_MODIFIED,
  url: new URL("/colophon/", site.url).href,
};
---

<PageLayout
  register="reference"
  title="Colophon"
  kicker="Reference"
  intro="How the machine layer of this site works."
  description={description}
>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      Most of this site is written by hand. A measurable slice of it is not: the daily digest, the
      audio that accompanies it, parts of the research library, and the freshness data behind the
      concepts graph are produced by pipelines that run on a schedule, commit their output to the
      site's git repository, and deploy through the same build as every hand-written page. This
      page documents those pipelines: what runs, what it reads, what gates its output, and where
      the results surface. Every page a pipeline produces carries a register stamp identifying it
      as machine output; the selection criteria and all of the code are by Stephanie Jarmak.
    </p>

    <h2 id="digest">Daily digest generation</h2>
    <p>
      The digest is written by a scheduled job, not a person. A local cron entry
      (<code>scripts/digest/run.sh</code>) runs on two cadences and three tracks: daily issues
      cover roughly the last 36 hours in five to seven items, weekly issues cover seven days in
      twelve to fifteen, and the track sets the scope (specialized issues go deep on this site's
      core topics, general issues round up the wider agentic field, curated issues assemble items
      picked by hand).
    </p>
    <p>
      Each run builds a prompt from a per-track template and hands it to a headless Claude session
      through <code>claude-auto</code>, a router that picks the least-loaded of several accounts so
      a rate-capped one does not kill the run. The session selects source items through the
      code-intel-copilot MCP server, a feed of recent code-intelligence material it can search
      lexically and semantically, aggregate, and pull individual items from, and then writes the
      issue.
    </p>
    <p>
      Before an issue reaches the content collection it passes a publish gate
      (<code>scripts/digest/publish-digest.mjs</code>). The gate validates the issue against a
      schema mirroring the site's digest collection and derives the issue's provenance register
      from its origin: automated issues publish as <em>generated</em>, hand-curated ones as
      <em>hybrid</em>, and an issue claiming a register its origin contradicts is refused before
      anything is written. A concept gate then resolves each topic facet against the concepts
      alias table described below: an interactive publish refuses to write anything when none of
      an issue's facets resolve, a cron publish warns and proceeds, and in either mode any facets
      left unresolved are recorded in the issue's frontmatter, with cron runs additionally filing
      alias proposals for later review.
    </p>
    <p>
      A repeat-coverage guard (<code>scripts/digest/recent-coverage.mjs</code>) keeps consecutive
      issues from re-covering the same material. It compares normalized item URLs (fragments
      dropped, tracking parameters stripped) against recent issues of the same cadence and track,
      seven days back for daily issues and twenty-eight for weekly. The generator receives the
      do-not-repeat list up front, and the publish gate independently refuses any repeated URL, so
      the guard holds even when the model ignores its instructions. Published issues appear on
      <a href="/digest">the digest</a> and accumulate in <a href="/digest/archive">the archive</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tts">Text-to-speech</h2>
    <p>
      Issues that ship with audio are narrated by Kokoro-82M, a small open-weight TTS model that
      runs entirely on local hardware; no text leaves the machine and no external API is involved.
      A Node script (<code>scripts/digest/tts-render.mjs</code>) strips the issue's markdown down
      to speakable text and splits it into chunks of at most 4,000 characters on paragraph and
      sentence boundaries; a Python worker loads the model once and renders each chunk to WAV
      (voice <code>am_onyx</code>, at 0.85 speed), and ffmpeg concatenates the parts into a single
      24&nbsp;kHz mono MP3 committed under <code>/media/digests/</code> and served statically from
      the <a href="/digest">issue pages</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="knowledge-sync">Knowledge sync</h2>
    <p>
      A daily post-digest job (<code>scripts/knowledge/sync-knowledge.sh</code>) decides whether
      papers cited by that day's specialized digest belong in the site's research libraries or
      thematic explorers. The two destinations get different treatment: library additions are
      written as proposals for human review and never touch NASA ADS directly, while explorer
      additions are applied to the in-repo explorer files together with a grounded synthesis of
      each paper, read through the SciX MCP tools against the NASA ADS corpus.
    </p>
    <p>
      The library data itself is pulled by <code>scripts/knowledge/build_libraries.py</code> from
      curated NASA ADS biblib libraries, enriched with metadata from the ADS search API, and
      committed as a JSON snapshot; the site builds from the committed file, so what visitors see
      in <a href="/library">the library</a> is the state of the libraries at the last sync, not a
      live query. A companion script validates the explorer files (self-contained thematic
      literature maps) and assembles them into the combined dataset behind the same page. At the
      end of every sync cycle, whether it did work, found nothing to do, or failed, a rider step
      updates the sync heartbeat described below.
    </p>

    <h2 id="concepts">Concepts registry and graph</h2>
    <p>
      The registry is 36 curated concepts, each a Markdown entry under
      <code>src/content/concepts/</code> with a label, a set of aliases, and a definition. An
      alias table maps every normalized label and alias to its canonical slug, and the build fails
      if two entries claim the same name; this same table is what the digest's concept gate
      resolves topic facets against, so the digest and the graph share one vocabulary.
    </p>
    <p>
      A concept's evidence is never stored, only derived at build time: digest topic facets,
      explorer section themes, per-paper theme branches, and a committed assignments file join
      into per-concept evidence lanes covering digest issues, explorer sections, papers, notes,
      and threads. The <a href="/prototypes/concepts">concepts prototype</a> renders the result as
      an interactive graph with a detail panel showing each concept's evidence and a freshness
      line summarizing how recently it was tagged.
    </p>

    <h2 id="retrieval">Embeddings and retrieval lanes</h2>
    <p>
      The repository also carries build-time retrieval machinery over the site's knowledge
      corpus: a semantic lane (embedding dot product), a lexical lane (BM25), a graph-adjacency
      lane, and a recency lane, fused with reciprocal-rank fusion into a single ranking. The
      embeddings behind the semantic lane are baked once by
      <code>scripts/knowledge/build_embeddings.py</code> using
      <code>sentence-transformers/all-mpnet-base-v2</code> (768 dimensions, L2-normalized) and
      committed to the repository, so retrieval reflects the corpus as it stood at authoring
      time; nothing runs in the browser and the deployed site calls no model endpoint. The lanes
      are implemented and exercised by the site's test suite, but no page currently renders their
      fused output as a related-items list.
    </p>

    <h2 id="heartbeat">Sync heartbeat</h2>
    <p>
      Because several of these pipelines run unattended, the repository carries a committed
      heartbeat file (<code>src/data/knowledge/concept-sync-status.json</code>) recording when
      each pipeline step last ran, which flags were set, how many concept proposals are waiting in
      the backlog and how old the oldest is, and what fraction of digest topic facets currently
      resolve to registered concepts. It stores counts and timestamps only, never proposal
      contents. Both the digest publish gate and the knowledge-sync rider write it, and the
      <a href="/prototypes/concepts">concepts prototype</a> renders it as a sync-heartbeat panel,
      so pipeline drift is visible on the page the pipelines feed.
    </p>

    <p>
      Everything above runs from code committed to this site's repository and deploys through the
      same continuous-integration checks as the hand-written pages; where a description here and
      the code disagree, the code is current.
    </p>
  </div>
</PageLayout>

<script type="application/ld+json" is:inline set:html={JSON.stringify(techArticleLd)} />
